# De Gemeten Stad — ontology v0 (TBox)
# ============================================================================
# The graph model from IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md §3, promoted from Spike E.
#
# The graph is deliberately THIN. It carries identity, relations, provenance,
# and confidence only. Every number and geometry — tree counts, polygons,
# replant fractions, stamdiameter — lives in the Postgres/PostGIS value store,
# NEVER as an RDF literal. A class or property here that looks like it wants a
# number is a modelling error: it should reference an entity that resolves to
# the value store instead.
#
# Namespaces (settled, design.md): the RDF base is gemetenstad.nl (NO hyphen —
# the Spike E probes used gemeten-stad.nl, which this committed model supersedes).
#   gs:   http://gemetenstad.nl/ns#    — terms (this file)
#   data: http://gemetenstad.nl/id/    — instance identities
#   run:  http://gemetenstad.nl/run/   — run-stamped named graphs + provenance
# ============================================================================

@prefix gs:   <http://gemetenstad.nl/ns#> .
@prefix data: <http://gemetenstad.nl/id/> .
@prefix run:  <http://gemetenstad.nl/run/> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix dct:  <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<http://gemetenstad.nl/ns>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "De Gemeten Stad ontology (v0)"@en ;
    dct:description
        "TBox for auditing Amsterdam government interventions against public observation data.  v0 covers vertical 1: the tree felling/replant (herplantplicht) lifecycle.  Thin graph (identity + relations + provenance + confidence); values live in Postgres."@en ;
    owl:versionInfo "0" .

# ============================================================================
# 1. Classes — the Intervention–Place–Claim–Observation spine (§3)
# ============================================================================

gs:Intervention
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Intervention"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "A government act audited against reality. In v0: a tree-felling permit  (kap-omgevingsvergunning). Its stable identity anchors everything else;  it is located at a Place, may be part of a Project, and makes Claims.  Numbers (how many trees) live in the value store, not here."@en .

gs:Place
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Place"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "The smallest area an Intervention can be confidently placed in: a BAG object, a project polygon, a postcode-6, or a gebieden buurt/wijk (whichever is smallest by area and clears the confidence threshold — §4). A Place is a first-class entity: identity is its code (BAG object id / gebieden code); it MAY carry a common name via rdfs:label and its containing area via gs:within, forming the buurt→wijk→stadsdeel skeleton the audit aggregates over. That skeleton (code + name + gs:within) is projected into the graph from the authoritative gebieden tables — the GEOMETRY itself stays in PostGIS (the value store), never as an RDF literal. Place names/hierarchy are NOT SKOS vocab concepts (a Place is recognized by geometry, not name)."@en .

gs:Project
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Project"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "A renewal/herstructurering programme several Interventions belong to (e.g.  'De Oranje Loper'). Sparse — present only for interventions that are part of one;  a one-off permit has no Project. Modelling it as an optional edge (not a column)  is exactly why the graph earns its place (§3)."@en .

gs:Claim
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Claim"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "The obligation derived once from an Intervention + the regulation: replant N trees  of diameter-class C at place L, by deadline D-or-unknown. Stable — derived from the  permit and the law, it does not itself evolve (the Assessment of it does)."@en .

gs:Observation
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Observation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "What the registry / world data actually shows against a Claim: the kapenherplant  felling and replant records, boomAanwezigheid, dates. A Claim is testedAgainst an  Observation. The observed counts live in the value store; the node carries identity."@en .

gs:AuditLink
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "AuditLink"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "The stable, write-once, timeless per-permit ANCHOR node — 'the coverage audit of  this permit', one per Intervention. Carries gs:coversIntervention to the audited  Intervention and never itself moves: the derived, time-varying coverage state (a  match's confidence/evidence/granularity or a no-source verdict) lives on its  gs:CoveragePeriod versions, opened/closed as re-derivation updates the picture (§3 D4,  the node-form valid-time mechanism via gs:versionOf). The anchor never carries  gs:confidence or gs:evidence directly."@en .

gs:CoveragePeriod
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "CoveragePeriod"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "A DERIVED, dated coverage state for one gs:AuditLink anchor — one period per  re-derived outcome, opened/closed via gs:versionOf its anchor plus  gs:validFrom/gs:validTo (the node-form valid-time mechanism, §3 D4). Carries  gs:evidence and, when a registry match was found, gs:linksObservation to the matched  gs:Observation + gs:confidence in [0,1] + gs:granularity (the place-resolution tier the  match rests on) + optional gs:caveat; or, when no match was found, gs:noSourceFound  true (a match was attempted and none found). Unlike the RDF-star annotation convention  used elsewhere (§3), gs:confidence and gs:evidence here are PLAIN properties directly on  the period node — the period node itself is the thing being annotated, so there is no  base edge to annotate over."@en .

gs:Assessment
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Assessment"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "A DERIVED verdict on a Claim's fulfilment at a point in time: {estimate none/partial/  fulfilled, timeliness, caveats, confidence}. The SCD-Type-2 fluent: each derive run  opens a new Assessment and closes the prior by stamping gs:validTo — NEVER overwritten,  so a now-fulfilled claim retains its earlier partial assessment in history (§3, D4)."@en .

gs:LegalStatusPeriod
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "LegalStatusPeriod"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "A period during which an Intervention held a given legal force (in-force → repealed/  amended). The second use of the state/period (SCD-Type-2) mechanism: valid-time on a  bundle of state that moves together. An amendment is a NEW decision entity linked to  the prior, never an in-place edit (§3)."@en .

gs:Caveat
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Caveat"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "A named, controlled reason a fuzzy edge is not exact — what we explicitly do NOT know.  Attached to an edge via gs:caveat. The four v0 caveat terms are individuals below."@en .

# ============================================================================
# 2. Object properties — the spine's relations (§3)
# ============================================================================

gs:locatedAt
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "located at"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:Intervention ;
    rdfs:range  gs:Place ;
    rdfs:comment
        "Places an Intervention on the map. A FUZZY edge: it MUST carry a gs:confidence +  gs:evidence RDF-star annotation (see the uncertainty convention below). Required  (minCount 1, shapes.ttl)."@en .

gs:partOfProject
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "part of project"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:Intervention ;
    rdfs:range  gs:Project ;
    rdfs:comment
        "Optional and sparse — present only for interventions belonging to a renewal project.  Its absence is valid (the shapes do not require it)."@en .

gs:within
    a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:TransitiveProperty ;
    rdfs:label "within"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:Place ;
    rdfs:range  gs:Place ;
    rdfs:comment "Spatial containment between Places: a buurt gs:within a wijk gs:within a stadsdeel. Forms the aggregation skeleton the audit rolls obligations/assessments up (tree → buurt → wijk → stadsdeel, §3) — a graph traversal (SPARQL gs:within+ property path), no cross-store join. Transitive. Seeded from the gebieden tables (ligtinwijkid etc.); the value store keeps the geometry for spatial containment tests, the graph keeps the resolved membership for rollup." .

gs:claims
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "claims"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:Intervention ;
    rdfs:range  gs:Claim ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates an Intervention to the obligation(s) it gives rise to."@en .

gs:testedAgainst
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "tested against"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:Claim ;
    rdfs:range  gs:Observation ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a Claim (obligation) to the Observation (registry reality) that tests it."@en .

gs:coversIntervention
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "covers intervention"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:AuditLink ;
    rdfs:range  gs:Intervention ;
    rdfs:comment
        "Relates a gs:AuditLink anchor to the permit-side gs:Intervention it is the coverage  audit of. Required (minCount 1, shapes.ttl) — an anchor with no audited Intervention is  meaningless."@en .

gs:linksObservation
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "links observation"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:CoveragePeriod ;
    rdfs:range  gs:Observation ;
    rdfs:comment
        "Relates a matched gs:CoveragePeriod to the registry-side gs:Observation the permit was  found to cover. Present only on the 'matched' outcome branch — absent when  gs:noSourceFound is true (shapes.ttl gs:CoveragePeriodShape, sh:xone)."@en .

gs:granularity
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "granularity"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:CoveragePeriod ;
    rdfs:comment
        "The place-resolution tier the matched coverage link rests on — one of gs:address,  gs:postcode, or gs:buurt (finest to coarsest, §4). Present only on a matched  gs:CoveragePeriod; its value is controlled (sh:in, shapes.ttl)."@en .

# The activity/species/status of an intervention or its trees resolve to SKOS concepts in
# vocab.ttl. Identity stays in codes/IRIs, never names (§3). Controlled by the shapes.

gs:activity
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "activity"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:Intervention ;
    rdfs:range  skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:comment
        "The measure carried out — resolves to the single felling concept (verplanten ≡ vellen,  Spike C / Bomenverordening art. 1k) or the replant concept in the activity scheme."@en .

gs:species
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "species"@en ;
    rdfs:range  skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:comment
        "The tree species — resolves to a concept in the species scheme (vocab.ttl), seeded  city-wide from stamgegevens.soortnaamTop (Dutch top level) with soortnaam cultivars  as skos:broader children."@en .

gs:status
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "status"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:Observation ;
    rdfs:range  skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:comment "The observed fulfilment status of an Observation — resolves to a status concept in vocab.ttl (the kapenherplant boomAanwezigheid axis, Spike C). Controlled by the shapes." .

gs:caveat
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "caveat"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:Caveat ;   # NB: written as an RDF-star annotation on a fuzzy edge, so the
                              # subject at write-time is the quoted triple, not a plain node.
    rdfs:range  gs:Caveat ;
    rdfs:comment
        "Flags an explicit, controlled reason a fuzzy edge (locatedAt / AuditLink) is not exact.  Written as an RDF-star annotation alongside gs:confidence:  :i gs:locatedAt :p {| gs:confidence 0.6 ; gs:caveat gs:timeMismatch |}.  Its value MUST be one of the four gs:Caveat individuals below (controlled, shapes.ttl)."@en .

gs:noSourceFound
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "no source found"@en ;
    rdfs:range  xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:comment
        "Marks a gs:CoveragePeriod as a NO-SOURCE outcome: a registry match was attempted for  the audited Intervention and none was found (value true). Present only on the  'no-source' outcome branch — absent (never false) when a gs:linksObservation match  exists (shapes.ttl gs:CoveragePeriodShape, sh:xone)."@en .

# The three controlled gs:granularity values — the place-resolution tier a matched coverage
# link rests on (finest to coarsest, §4). Kept minimal (label only, no Granularity class or
# SKOS scheme): a fixed, small, model-level enum bound by shapes.ttl (sh:in).

gs:address
    rdfs:label "address"@en .

gs:postcode
    rdfs:label "postcode"@en .

gs:buurt
    rdfs:label "buurt"@en .

# ============================================================================
# 3. Uncertainty convention — RDF-star annotation terms (§3, D2)
# ============================================================================
# HARD MODEL RULE: confidence + evidence are written with the RDF-star ANNOTATION
# form  {| … |}  — NEVER the bare quoted form  << … >> .
#
#   :i gs:locatedAt :p {| gs:confidence 0.7 ; gs:evidence "place+time match" |} .
#
# The annotation form asserts the base edge (:i gs:locatedAt :p) AND stores the
# annotation — so structural SHACL (a plain property path) still sees the edge,
# and the annotation is readable via SPARQL-star:
#
#   SELECT ?c WHERE { << :i gs:locatedAt :p >> gs:confidence ?c }
#
# The bare  << … >>  form does NOT assert the base edge (Spike E, verified with
# riot), so structural SHACL would not see it. Do not use it. This rule is
# enforced by the confidence-presence sh:sparql constraint in shapes.ttl.

gs:confidence
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "confidence"@en ;
    rdfs:range  xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:comment
        "How sure we are of the fuzzy edge it annotates, in [0,1] (range enforced by shapes.ttl). 1.0 denotes an exact resolution; any value < 1.0 marks a non-exact edge, which the shapes then require to carry a gs:caveat naming why (see shapes.ttl caveat-presence). Written as an RDF-star annotation; read via SPARQL-star. Shown in the UI as 'how sure'."@en .

gs:evidence
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "evidence"@en ;
    rdfs:range  xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment
        "A short human-readable statement of what the confidence rests on (e.g. 'exact BAG address  match at permit valid-time'). Written as an RDF-star annotation on the same fuzzy edge."@en .

# ============================================================================
# 4. Temporal convention — valid-time terms (§3, D4)
# ============================================================================
# Separate stable identity from time-varying state; stamp validity only on state.
#   • Identity is timeless — one stable IRI per entity, never changed or deleted.
#   • Immutable facts stay UN-stamped — a publication date, a felling date are
#     timeless facts; recording WHEN something happened is not the fact evolving.
#     Over-stamping is an anti-pattern (every query then drags time filters).
#   • Evolving state carries valid time via ONE of two mechanisms, chosen by shape:
#       (a) RDF-star statement annotation on a single evolving fact that also
#           carries confidence — e.g. a legal-status or location link;
#       (b) a state/period NODE (gs:Assessment, gs:LegalStatusPeriod) — the SCD-2
#           fluent — when several attributes move together and periods are queried
#           as objects. Opened/closed by writing a new node and stamping validTo on
#           the prior; NEVER overwritten.
#
# Transaction-time ("when did WE record it") is NOT modelled with these terms — it
# is PROV: each load/derive run writes a run-stamped named graph and records a
# prov:Activity with prov:generatedAtTime in the run:_provenance named graph (D5).

gs:validFrom
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "valid from"@en ;
    rdfs:range  xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment
        "Start of the valid-time (world-time) interval of an evolving state. On an Assessment/  LegalStatusPeriod node, or as an RDF-star annotation on a single evolving fact.  Absent on immutable facts."@en .

gs:validTo
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "valid to"@en ;
    rdfs:range  xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment
        "End of the valid-time interval; absent/open while the state is current. Stamping it is  how a state/period node is CLOSED when a successor opens — history is retained, never  overwritten."@en .

gs:versionOf
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "version of"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "Points from a period node to its stable series anchor IRI. A period node carries a plain  gs:validFrom + gs:versionOf <anchor>; the writer keeps exactly one open period per anchor,  closing the prior open period when a new one appears — history retained, never overwritten.  The anchor is a stable, domain-chosen IRI identifying the series (timeless; never itself  carries validFrom/validTo). This is the node-form valid-time mechanism (see  docs/RDF_STAR_RELATIONSHIPS.md), distinct from the RDF-star refinable-edge form  (gs:confidence/gs:evidence/gs:caveat annotations), which is transaction-time only and never  carries valid-time. Range deliberately unconstrained — any IRI can anchor a series; a  domain model's shapes constrain it further if needed."@en .

gs:active
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "active"@en ;
    rdfs:domain gs:Place ;
    rdfs:range  xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:comment
        "Whether a Place is currently a live area in the source gebieden register (true) or a  soft-deleted/deprecated one (false) — a status FLAG, not a value (the geometry stays in  PostGIS). This is EVOLVING STATE, not the Place's timeless identity: the statement is  written via the RDF-star annotation mechanism above, carrying gs:validFrom/gs:validTo  (<< :place gs:active true >> gs:validFrom … ; gs:validTo … ), so an area going inactive  supersedes the prior interval rather than overwriting it. The Place's IRI, rdfs:label,  and gs:within remain stable and timeless across the transition."@en .

# ============================================================================
# 5. Caveat terms — the controlled set of non-exactness flags (§3, D3; task 1.6)
# ============================================================================
# Fixed model enum (small, not data-derived): the four reasons a fuzzy edge may be
# non-exact. Values of gs:caveat. Bound by the shapes (sh:in) to exactly these.

gs:unresolvedLocation
    a gs:Caveat ;
    rdfs:label "unresolved location"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "The reference address did not exist in BAG (e.g. demolished in a renewal area), so the  location was resolved by fallback rather than an exact BAG object match (§4)."@en .

gs:timeMismatch
    a gs:Caveat ;
    rdfs:label "time mismatch"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "No BAG voorkomen was valid at the intervention's date, so the location was resolved  against a voorkomen from another time and the time alignment is uncertain (§3, §8)."@en .

gs:weakLink
    a gs:Caveat ;
    rdfs:label "weak link"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "The permit↔trees AuditLink rests on coarse or partial evidence (e.g. buurt-level only,  count/time window loose) — the join is plausible but not strong."@en .

gs:transplantOrigin
    a gs:Caveat ;
    rdfs:label "transplant origin"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "The felling was a verplanten (transplant): legally a velling (Bomenverordening art. 1k,  Spike C) and counted in the felling total, but the tree may persist at a new site — so  the 'removal' is qualified."@en .

gs:countUnknown
    a gs:Caveat ;
    rdfs:label "count unknown"@en ;
    rdfs:comment
        "The permit's stated tree count is unknown (Phase 1 does not extract permit-text  counts), so the count axis of the coverage score contributes nothing — the matched link  rests on place + time alone."@en .
